Tier B (bug fix) — find_missing.py:61: use the current row's genome, not a leaked loop variable

`mem` is computed from `sid`, which is not defined in this loop. It leaks from the loop
that ends at line 53 (`for sid in sorted(G.nodes[node]['seqIDs'])`), so after that loop it
holds the lexicographically last seqID of the last node satisfying the merged test. `mem`
is therefore one arbitrary, constant genome index for every row of gene_data.csv.

Effect: every merged-node DNA sequence piles into a single `merged_nodes[mem]` bucket, and
every other genome receives an empty dict from the defaultdict. `search_gff` consumes this
as `merged_nodes[member]`, one genome at a time, and uses it to decide whether to recover
the *full extent* of a merged gene by alignment (lines 269-283) instead of trusting the
short annotated fragment bounds. So for all genomes but one, that recovery silently never
runs: less seq_coverage is marked, fewer overlaps are detected, and members that Panaroo
intended to drop via remove_member_from_node survive into gene_presence_absence.csv.

Read as unintentional because the data structure contradicts the assignment: merged_nodes
is a `defaultdict(dict)` keyed by member and consumed per genome, a shape that is pointless
unless `mem` varied. Every other read in the block uses `line[2]`, and `line[2]` is exactly
the clustering ID whose first field is the genome index — the same idiom used at line 77
(`member = int(sid.split("_")[0])`).

MEASURED EFFECT (strict mode, PYTHONHASHSEED=0, upstream e96b497). Stock -> patched:

                        smoke (4 x 3 contigs)   ci (4 full M. tuberculosis genomes)
  total gene clusters   3732 -> 3731            5118 -> 5116
  core genes             746 -> 746             5097 -> 5096
  shell genes           2986 -> 2985              21 -> 20
  presence calls        9838 -> 9837           20431 -> 20428
  refound genes           18 -> 18                56 -> 56
  wall clock           53.9s -> 53.1s          65.3s -> 66.0s

  pre_filt_graph.gml is byte-identical on both, as expected: find_missing runs after it is
  written. gene_presence_absence.Rtab differs on both, so this is a structural change to the
  pangenome, not a rendering one. On `ci`, gene_data.csv and combined_*_CDS.fasta also
  differ -- find_missing appends refound records to them.

Direction matches the analysis: with the fix, more seq_coverage is marked, more overlaps are
detected, and a node that loses its last member is deleted. The effect is small but real:
1-2 clusters out of 3700-5100.

I predicted this would be materially slower, because the fix un-masks the merged-extent
search that is currently skipped for all but one genome. That did not happen at either
scale (<1% either way). The prediction was wrong; do not budget for it, but re-measure at
higher genome counts before assuming it stays free.

Status: OFF by default. See PORTING_PLAN.md 6.1 Tier B.

